/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 4:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Explain Variable Scope and the LEGB Rule in Python, detailing global, nonlocal, and closure variable bindings.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Variable resolution in Python follows the strict LEGB \(Local, Enclosing, Global, Built-in\)) Tj
T*
(   rule:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   The Hierarchy:) Tj
T*
(   1. Local \(L\): Names assigned within the active function body.) Tj
T*
(   2. Enclosing \(E\): Names in outer enclosing functions from inside out \(closures\).) Tj
T*
(   3. Global \(G\): Module-level variables defined at script top.) Tj
T*
(   4. Built-in \(B\): Pre-loaded Python names \(`len`, `range`, `Exception`\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   The `global` vs `nonlocal` Keywords:) Tj
T*
(   By default, any assignment `x = val` inside a function creates a new Local variable `x`. If you) Tj
T*
(   wish to mutate an existing outer variable:) Tj
T*
(   - `global var`: Binds assignment directly to the top-level module namespace.) Tj
T*
(   - `nonlocal var`: Binds assignment to the nearest enclosing non-global function scope) Tj
T*
(   \(essential for mutable counters in closures\).) Tj
T*
ET

