(   [Problem Statement]: Explain Closures and the Late-Binding Problem in Python loops, providing mathematical explanations and the default argument solution.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Python closures bind variables by reference to the variable environment, NOT by value snapshot:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   The Late-Binding Trap:) Tj
T*
(   ```python) Tj
T*
(   def create_multipliers\(\):) Tj
T*
(   return [lambda x: x * i for i in range\(4\)]) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   multipliers = create_multipliers\(\)) Tj
T*
(   # Expected: [0, 2, 4, 6] for x = 2) Tj
T*
(   # Actual output:) Tj
T*
(   print\([m\(2\) for m in multipliers]\) # Outputs: [6, 6, 6, 6]!) Tj
T*
(   ```) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   Root Cause:) Tj
T*
(   All four lambda functions close over the single variable identifier `i`. The variable `i` is) Tj
T*
(   looked up when the lambda is CALLED, not when it was defined. By the time `m\(2\)` is invoked,) Tj
T*
(   the loop has completed and `i` equals `3` \($2 \\times 3 = 6$\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   The Default Argument Solution:) Tj
T*
(   `return [lambda x, i=i: x * i for i in range\(4\)]`) Tj
T*
(   Default arguments are evaluated and bound at DEFINITION time, permanently snapshotting each) Tj
T*
(   distinct integer into its local parameter.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
