(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the Global Interpreter Lock \(GIL\) in CPython and its impact on CPU-bound vs I/O-bound multithreaded Python scripts.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   The Global Interpreter Lock \(GIL\) is a mutual exclusion lock used by CPython to prevent) Tj
T*
(   multiple native OS threads from executing Python bytecode simultaneously:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   Why the GIL Exists:) Tj
T*
(   CPython's reference counting memory management is not thread-safe. Without the GIL, concurrent) Tj
T*
(   threads modifying object reference counts would cause race conditions, memory corruption, and) Tj
T*
(   fatal segmentation faults.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   Impact on Engineering Applications:) Tj
T*
(   1. I/O-Bound Tasks \(Serial Communications, Sockets, File I/O\):) Tj
T*
(   - Python threads excel here! When a thread waits for a network packet or serial byte from an) Tj
T*
(   oscilloscope, it releases the GIL, allowing other threads to run concurrently.) Tj
T*
(   2. CPU-Bound Tasks \(Heavy Numerical Signal Processing, FFTs, Matrix Math\):) Tj
T*
(   - Multithreading in pure Python yields ZERO speedup on multi-core CPUs due to GIL contention.) Tj
T*
(   - Solutions: Use the `multiprocessing` module \(spawns separate Python processes each with its) Tj
T*
(   own GIL\), or use C-accelerated vectorized libraries \(NumPy, SciPy\) that release the GIL during) Tj
T*
(   native SIMD array operations.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 21:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
ET

